WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.060
This episode is brought to you by Vincero
Watches.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.320
What’s up, Wisecrack.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.500
Jared again.

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:10.990
With the recent release of J.R.R.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:14.280
Tolkien’s final book
and the upcoming Middle-earth Amazon series

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.310
with a production budget of a half-a-billion
dollars, it’s a great time to be a Tolkien

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:20.310
fan.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.170
But while the growing body of work set in
Middle-earth means everything is always improving

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:30.839
for fantasy fans, Tolkien himself was of the
opposite opinion: everything is always getting

00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:32.320
worse.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.540
A self-avowed pessimist, Tolkien went so far
as to say that he didn’t believe history

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.390
would be anything more than one long defeat.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.150
This idea of defeat and decline is the very
quality that infuses his fiction with such

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.600
a sense of longing and nostalgia.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.170
But is progress really a pernicious myth,
or was Tolkien hitting that pipe-weed a little

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.280
too hard?

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.080
*laughter*
Well let’s crack open some dusty tomes and

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.850
find out in this Wisecrack Edition on J.R.R.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.310
Tolkien’s Philosophy of History.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.129
But before we get there, just a quick shoutout
to our sponsor Vincero.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:09.640
They’ve been massively supportive of us
and let us do cool stuff, like get super nerdy

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:10.870
about Tolkien.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:14.870
Right now I’m wearing their Chrono S, and
it’s helped me stop checking my phone every

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.580
ten minutes so I can stay on track making
more videos.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.900
They’ve got a new years sale happening right
now so, if you wanna set yourself apart and

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.740
step up your game, check out the link below
and watch until the end of the videa.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.501
They’ve got great watches for fair prices,
so thanks again to Vincero, and now, back

00:01:30.501 --> 00:01:31.501
to the show.

00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:33.310
Here’s a quick breakdown for the uninitiated.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.020
You’re probably aware of Tolkien’s most
famous works, Lord of the Rings and The Hobbit,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:43.380
or at least Peter Jackson’s phenomenal and
then just God-awful adaptations of them, respectively.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.909
But what you may not know is that Frodo and
Bilbo exist in an entire Tolkien Literary

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:49.890
Universe set in Middle Earth.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.390
The history of Middle Earth is long, and set
into distinct ages, with Lord of the Rings

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.300
occuring at the end of the third age and the
beginning of the 4th, but that’ll be important

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.300
in a little bit.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.299
These ages are, of course, fictional.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:05.840
But they also sync up with our own very real
timelines right around the period between

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.600
prehistory and history.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.659
In other words, Lord of the Rings happens
on Earth, just a really really long time ago.

00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:15.510
Which, for reasons I’ll get into, is very
important.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.569
But before we get into why Tolkien thought
everything is always getting worse, we have

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:21.159
to understand one thing.

00:02:21.159 --> 00:02:23.239
The dude loved history.

00:02:23.239 --> 00:02:28.029
In The Lost Road, his abandoned book about
time travel from our own era back to the Second

00:02:28.029 --> 00:02:32.959
Age of Middle-earth, Tolkien, through a clearly
autobiographical character, writes that his

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:36.389
most permanent mood
“had been since childhood the desire…

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:41.569
to see the lie of old and forgotten lands,
to behold ancient men walking, to hear their

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:45.489
languages as they spoke them, in the days
before, when tongues of forgotten lineage

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:48.220
were heard in the kingdoms long fallen….”

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.160
This deep passion for history shows in just
how much it pervades his writings.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.230
There are over six hundred references to the
history of Middle-earth in The Hobbit and

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:57.409
The Lord of the Rings.

00:02:57.409 --> 00:03:02.029
Tolkien’s own brush with world history had
an even more obvious influence on Middle-earth.

00:03:02.029 --> 00:03:06.559
Faithful servant Sam Gamgee was inspired by
the servants who helped officers such as Tolkien

00:03:06.559 --> 00:03:10.480
in the trenches of World War I, and Sam’s
journey through the blasted and barren land

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.030
of Mordor is an unmistakable image of No Man’s
Land.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.519
Tolkien’s interest extended beyond events
themselves and into the development of languages,

00:03:18.519 --> 00:03:19.719
literature, and legends.

00:03:19.719 --> 00:03:26.079
Punctuation: "I first started inventing languages
about uh, when I was 13 or 14, and I've never

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:27.079
stopped, really."

00:03:27.079 --> 00:03:31.370
In fact, the exploration of his artificial
languages were Tolkien’s main concern.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:35.599
Believe it or not, the entire history of Middle-earth
is really just a history of the development

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:37.870
of Tolkien’s constructed languages.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.749
Tolkien himself said, “The invention of
languages is the foundation.

00:03:41.749 --> 00:03:46.290
The 'stories' were made rather to provide
a world for the languages than the reverse.”

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.029
“What do you suppose that means?”

00:03:48.029 --> 00:03:51.819
“Well it’s quite simple, if you are a
friend you speak the password and the doors

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:52.819
will open”.

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:56.670
Tolkien was also fascinated by the historical
composition and transmission of the written

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.840
word, undoubtedly stemming from his work as
a specialist in medieval culture.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.239
The conceit for Lord of the Rings was that
it was his own English translation of a book

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:08.339
written by hobbits: The Red Book of Westmarch.

00:04:08.339 --> 00:04:13.120
Incidentally, this fictional book also has
a rich history with no less than five fictional

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.489
editions, tracing back through writings made
by Samwise, Frodo, and Bilbo.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:21.859
So the Lord of Rings isn’t meant to be read
as a perfect representation of historical

00:04:21.859 --> 00:04:26.210
events, but as stories that have been passed
down, altered, and inevitably, corrupted.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:30.590
"I wonder if people will ever say ‘Let’s
hear about Frodo and the Ring’ and they’ll

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.080
say ‘Yes it’s one of my favorite stories’".

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.520
So it’s safe to say that Tolkien was obsessed
with history and the process of writing it.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.949
But that’s what makes it so strange that
his thoughts about history were so radically

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:45.870
different from most contemporary historians
and laymen alike.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:50.150
Many philosophers of history – especially
those popular in Tolkien’s time – thought

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:54.530
the move from past to future represented some
kind of progress.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:58.699
Technologically, we’ve gotten the wheel,
the steam engine, and frozen pizza.

00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:03.360
And morally, most people would agree that
we became a more just society after the adoption

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.590
of the Bill of Rights,
and even more so after the end of slavery,

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.860
and again after the end of segregation.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:13.680
Martin Luther King summed up this thinking
when he said, “the arc of the moral universe

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.539
is long, but it bends toward justice.”

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:18.490
Tolkien vehemently disagreed.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:22.870
He thought that our technological progress
wasn’t entirely a good thing and that we

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:27.681
weren’t progressing morally whatsoever,
proclaiming in On Fairy-Stories “The way

00:05:27.681 --> 00:05:32.949
men were living in the twentieth century was
increasing in barbarity at an alarming rate….”

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:37.440
While Tolkien acknowledged that technology
grew increasingly complex, he mocked the idea

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.009
that industrialization represented an advancement
for society.

00:05:41.009 --> 00:05:45.979
He witnessed his beloved boyhood home of Sarehole
despoiled by the encroachment of men and machinery.

00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:50.220
It’s a scene which replays itself during
the Scouring of the Shire, in which trees

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:55.310
are needlessly uprooted to make room for ugly
rows of housing and a pleasant water mill

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:59.199
is replaced by one billowing black smoke all
over the countryside.

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:03.500
In The Lost Road he conflates the human kingdom
of Númenor’s industrialization with their

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:04.569
cultural decay.

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:09.889
“Our towers grow ever stronger and climb
ever higher, but beauty they leave behind

00:06:09.889 --> 00:06:14.539
upon the earth… men are ceasing to give
love or care for the making of other things

00:06:14.539 --> 00:06:16.409
for use or delight.”

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:21.969
In The Hobbit Tolkien claims that technological
development is a symptom of orcish thought,

00:06:21.969 --> 00:06:24.849
commenting
“It is not unlikely that [goblins] invented

00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:29.439
some of the machines that have since troubled
the world, especially the ingenious devices

00:06:29.439 --> 00:06:34.289
for killing large numbers of people at once,
for wheels and engines and explosions always

00:06:34.289 --> 00:06:35.909
delighted them”.

00:06:35.909 --> 00:06:40.090
Those wheels and engines were rolled out for
the first time in the form of tanks upon the

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:44.860
Western Front, where Tolkien at the Battle
of the Somme witnessed firsthand the goblin-work

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:48.310
wrought by the explosive power of modern artillery.

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:52.189
Despite the horrors of trench and chemical
warfare, many of Tolkien’s contemporaries

00:06:52.189 --> 00:06:57.069
at the time regarded the Great War as emblematic
not only of technological progress in the

00:06:57.069 --> 00:07:02.090
form of more innovative means of mass killing,
but as a necessary step in human history for

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:03.950
social progress too.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:06.389
Tolkien, always the pessimist, disagreed.

00:07:06.389 --> 00:07:11.449
He believed conflict to be inevitable, and
recounted years later that this idea arose

00:07:11.449 --> 00:07:15.939
as a reaction to the contemporary discussions
about a War to End All Wars.

00:07:15.939 --> 00:07:21.419
He didn’t believe such rhetoric during the
war, and disliked it even more after it.

00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:26.979
That conflict must be perpetual is part of
his idea of history as being cyclical.

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:31.539
Instead of there being a straight line from
a primitive past to a more civilized future,

00:07:31.539 --> 00:07:35.740
Tolkien envisions societies throughout the
ages all dealing with the same perennial set

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:39.669
of circumstances arising from unchanging human
nature.

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:42.169
"War will make corpses of us all."

00:07:42.169 --> 00:07:45.639
Many of the events in Middle Earth are meant
to be reminiscent of the past.

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:50.270
So we have Frodo Nine-Fingered in possession
of Sauron’s ring during the third age, aka

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:55.819
Lord of the Rings, which resembles another
amputee hero from the past, Beren One-Handed,

00:07:55.819 --> 00:08:00.260
who recovers a magic jewel from Sauron’s
former master in the first age.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:04.539
But Tolkien is no George Lucas, building a
second Death Star, or J.J. Abrams creating

00:08:04.539 --> 00:08:07.009
a third… and good god I hope not a fourth.

00:08:07.009 --> 00:08:11.530
The repetition of this motif is not the result
of a lazy author returning to the same well

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:13.350
for lack of an original idea.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:18.169
Rather, Frodo bears likeness to Beren because
the world they inhabit, though separated by

00:08:18.169 --> 00:08:22.300
thousands of years, is once again in similar
straights.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:28.189
history is repeating itself because it is
cyclical.

00:08:28.189 --> 00:08:36.580
"The Eagles are Coming” “The Eagles!

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:37.580
The Eagles are Coming!"

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:41.690
But the history of Middle-earth is not simply
cyclical, it’s cyclical but always getting

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:42.690
worse.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:46.120
These overarching cycles in the history of
Middle-earth were broken up into four distinct

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.480
ages that illustrate history as degradation.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:54.480
These bear conspicuous resemblance to Hesiod
and Ovid’s myths of the Ages of Man, in

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.760
which they established the now familiar pattern
of Golden, Silver, Bronze, and Iron ages.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.410
In mankind’s Golden Age, perfect people
lived in an Edenic State of Nature, not dissimilar

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:09.820
to the Elves in Tolkien’s First Age coming
to live among the gods in the earthly paradise.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:14.580
Then succeed the Silver and Bronze Ages, each
progressively worse for man and filled with

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.720
worse men, followed by the current Iron Age,
the most evil and unhappy yet.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:24.900
Likewise, across the ages of Middle-earth
there is an overriding entropy, each cycle

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.690
less mythic and more mundane.

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:32.310
There is a grandeur in the past that can be
echoed in the later ages, but never fully

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:33.310
recaptured.

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:37.560
"You are a lesser son of greater sires."

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.770
Consider Rome, aka the City on Seven hills,
which Caesar Augustus bragged he left as a

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.040
city of marble.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:45.570
Yes, the real ROME.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.230
Remember Tolkien’s history happens before
our own real history.

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:53.700
To drive the point home, we learn of the many
cities that preceded Rome, also are adorned

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.580
with marble and have seven of something.

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:00.350
But the further back in time we go, the more
grandeur we see.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:04.670
In the Third Age, it was the seven-leveled
city of Minas Tirith in Gondor.

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:08.890
In the Second Age, there was the city of Rómenna,
from which Tolkien implies that Rome derives

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:09.890
its name.

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:15.060
In the First Age, there was the original and
oldest, the seven-gated city of Gondolin.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:20.330
But as grand as we know Rome was in its heyday,
Tolkien would say it had nothing on Minis

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:22.900
Tirith during the reign of King Elessar.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:26.381
And he would have said, “Yeah, but this
ain’t nothing compared to Númenor in its

00:10:26.381 --> 00:10:29.830
Golden Age” (though he’d probably say
it slightly more regally).

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:33.280
And King Elros of Númenor would’ve said
to that “Y’all should hear my dad talk

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:38.030
about Gondolin when Turgon was king, before
those dragons burned it all down to the ground!”

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:39.690
(Again, more regally.)

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:43.280
The point being, for Tolkien, older equals
better.

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:47.430
Having the same pattern repeat time and again
throughout the cycles of history, but becoming

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:52.040
a progressively poorer and poorer copy, is
his way of illustrating that point.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:56.660
"The blood of Numenor is all but spent, its
pride and dignity forgotten!"

00:10:56.660 --> 00:11:00.020
Tolkien’s portrayal of history can be partially
attributed to aesthetics.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:04.320
Tolkien wished to imbue his work with a Medieval
flavor, and the Medieval period is primarily

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:07.590
viewed as the ruins of a more glorious antiquity.

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:11.160
Because in the history of Middle-earth each
age is inferior to the one which preceded

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:16.400
it, its whole history is imbibed with a nostalgia
for a past that can never be recovered.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:20.930
"Long have I desired to look upon the kings
of old.

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:23.490
My kin."

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:28.060
But it’s also, as we mentioned, a direct
challenge to the Myth of Progress.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:33.260
Instead he favors looking backwards and appreciating
past, perennial, and even eternal matters.

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:37.520
It’s for this reason that the noble knights
of Gondor always look Westward before eating

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:38.520
each meal.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:43.010
“We look towards Númenor that was, and
beyond to Elvenhome that is, and to that which

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:45.440
is beyond Elvenhome and ever will be.”

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.690
His friend CS Lewis coined the term “chronological
snobbery” to describe those who had an irrational

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.980
preference for anything new over anything
old.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:55.770
But Tolkien was no mere debbie downer.

00:11:55.770 --> 00:12:00.580
He had an actual philosophy of history that
he espoused, one which he and Lewis shared

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:03.150
with the medieval minds they spent their careers
studying.

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:07.950
Middle-earth and the real world are in constant
decline for Tolkien because of the way he

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:11.310
understands the fundamental nature of reality.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:16.440
Tolkien and Lewis believed that Evil was real,
not merely some idea or social construct,

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:18.660
but a real and powerful force.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:22.000
Indeed, the most powerful force in the world.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:27.080
Sometimes Tolkien would portray Evil as personalized,
as with Sauron, or with Sauron’s master

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:32.190
from the First Age, Morgoth, who was himself
more powerful than all the forces of good

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:33.670
in the world combined.

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:39.350
But Tolkien also portrayed Evil as an impersonal
force, something even the most pure and innocent

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:42.110
hearts were capable of being bent towards.

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:47.420
Gandalf refuses the Ring for that very reason,
knowing he could and would be corrupted.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:52.070
And it’s worth remembering that when he
stood at the Cracks of Doom, Frodo failed;

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:57.860
he abandoned his mission, gave into temptation,
and took the Ring for himself.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:08.300
“The ring is mine” “No”
Tolkien called the earth “Morgoth’s Ring,”

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:12.950
just as Sauron poured most of his potency
into the One Ring, his master Morgoth poured

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:16.640
his spirit throughout the earth and everything
else in existence.

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:21.610
So whereas particularized evils like Sauron
could be addressed, there is a primordial

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:25.500
evil in existence that will remain until the
earth itself is destroyed.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:30.070
It is because of this belief that he stated
“I do not expect ‘history’ to be anything

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:32.270
but a ‘long defeat.’”

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:36.390
This was what Tolkien originally envisioned
as the ending for his Book of Lost Tales.

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:41.630
“And now is the end of fair times come very
nigh, and behold, all the beauty that yet

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:47.140
was on the earth – fragments of unimagined
loveliness... now goeth it all up in smoke.”

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:51.750
The same despondent pessimism is best surmised
by Frodo as he lay dying on the cliffside

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:52.750
of Mount Doom.

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.030
“It’s like things are in the world.

00:13:55.030 --> 00:13:56.030
Hope fails.

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:57.030
An end comes.

00:13:57.030 --> 00:13:59.510
We have only a little time to wait now.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:03.500
We are lost in ruin and downfall, and there
is no escape.”

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:08.180
But of course Frodo lives and the ring, the
stand-in for the inherent evil of the world,

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:09.310
is destroyed.

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:10.990
So what gives?

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:14.810
Was Tolkien chickening-out from taking his
views of history and evil to their logical

00:14:14.810 --> 00:14:15.810
conclusion?

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:19.380
Did he feel compelled by the conventions of
the genre to have a happy ending?

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:20.800
Well that would certainly make sense.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:24.770
After all, he did write “I would venture
to assert that all complete fairy-stories

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:30.060
must have [the Consolation of the Happy Ending]…
the eucatastrophic tale is the true form of

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.690
the fairy-tale, and its highest function.”

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:38.570
Of all the many words in the many languages
that Tolkien invented, this word, ‘eucatastrophe,’

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:40.800
is undoubtedly the most important.

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:45.860
He defines it “in its fairy-tale setting,
a sudden and miraculous grace: never to be

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:47.370
counted on to recur.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:53.070
It does not deny the existence of dyscatastrophe,
of sorrow and failure...it denies (in the

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:58.810
face of much evidence, if you will) universal
final defeat… giving a fleeting glimpse

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:02.060
of Joy, Joy beyond the walls of the world.”

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:05.930
So basically- everything will continue to
go to shit, but there will be a sudden change

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:09.530
towards good fortune that works against this
dire conclusion.

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:12.590
Now, “Beyond the walls of the world” here
is telling.

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:15.920
Tolkien alludes to another force, as Gandalf
hints to Frodo,

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:20.660
"There are other forces at work in this world
besides the will of evil.

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:28.170
Bilbo was meant to find the ring.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:29.520
In which case you also were meant to have
it.

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:30.520
And that is an encouraging thought."

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:33.410
Later Agent Smith, doing his best Morpheus
impression, elaborates,

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:38.690
“You have come and are met here, in the
very nick of time, by chance, so it seems.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:40.230
Yet it is not so.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:44.910
Believe rather that it is ordered that we,
who sit here, and none others, must now find

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:47.030
council for the perils of the world.”

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:50.190
So something is driving history toward a goal.

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:53.370
This kind of eucatastrophe isn’t limited
to fictional stories.

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:58.220
Tolkien thought that real History “contains
a fairy-story, or a story of a larger kind

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:01.230
which embraces all the essence of fairy-stories.”

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:06.880
As such, Tolkien envisioned the End of History,
as being likewise eucatastrophic; that though

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:11.170
history would be nothing more than “the
long defeat,” it would ultimately have a

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:15.340
happy ending as unexpected as the eagles arriving
at Mount Doom.

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:19.060
Ultimately, this was grounded in what Tolkien
called Estel.

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:23.570
More than merely being Aragorn’s elvish
name, Estel was one of the two elvish words

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:26.590
meaning “hope” which also meant “trust.”

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:30.130
All throughout life Tolkien looked around
at the world, and between the scouring of

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:34.670
his own boyhood shire, his living through
a veritable Mordor in the trenches of the

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:37.680
Western Front,
and later while writing Lord of the Rings

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:42.750
witnessing the rise of real dark lords in
Hitler and Stalin, Tolkien had no reason to

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:45.440
look up and reasonably expect good.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.880
And yet through it all he never faltered in
Estel, in trust.

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:55.810
Just as Tolkien’s writing awakened an appreciation
of the past among the chronological snobs

00:16:55.810 --> 00:17:01.670
that had been enamored by modernity, so too
do his myths stir something in us that certainly

00:17:01.670 --> 00:17:04.610
hopes for there to be real good in the end.

00:17:04.610 --> 00:17:09.020
When we read The Silmarillion, or The Hobbit,
or most especially Lord of the Rings, we are

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:14.880
all like Sam, in the black night in the enemy’s
own land, struck by the beauty of a white

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.220
star twinkling above him.

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:22.600
“There, peeping among the cloud-wrack above
a dark tor high up in the mountains, Sam saw

00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:24.810
a white star twinkle for a while.

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:30.720
The beauty of it smote his heart, as he looked
up out of the forsaken land, and hope returned

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:31.720
to him.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:36.230
For like a shaft, clear and cold, the thought
pierced him that in the end the Shadow was

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:45.490
only a small and passing thing: there was
light and high beauty forever beyond its reach.”

